    body: `<p>Six weeks ago, the received wisdom in Los Angeles was that this year's awards season was over before it began. Tomasz Wren's <em>The Glass Orchard</em> had the Palme d'Or, a star in Celeste Marlowe whose royal engagement guaranteed wall-to-wall coverage, and a distributor in Meridian Pictures with deep pockets and a long memory. Everyone else, it was assumed, was playing for nominations.</p>
<p>Then <em>Salt Year</em> happened. Kit Abernathy's quiet, devastating film about a teenage oyster farmer on the Maine coast premiered at Telluride on a Saturday morning in a half-empty theater. By Sunday night, the festival had added three screenings. By the following weekend, its tiny distributor, Hollow Oak, was fielding calls from agents who had never returned its emails.</p>
<p>This week, the film's momentum became impossible to ignore. Its limited release in New York and Los Angeles posted the highest per-screen average of any live-action film this year, and a closely watched poll of guild members placed Ines Okonkwo-Bell, its 24-year-old lead, within striking distance of Marlowe in the best-actress category.</p>
<blockquote><p>Nobody in this town wants a coronation. They want a race. And now they've got one.</p></blockquote>
<p>"Nobody in this town wants a coronation," says one awards strategist who has worked on four best-picture winners and is not involved with either film. "They want a race. They want to feel like their vote matters. <em>Salt Year</em> gives them that, and it gives them a discovery. Voters love to feel like they found someone first."</p>
<p>Okonkwo-Bell, who was working as a barista in Providence when Abernathy cast her from an open call, has handled the attention with a composure that belies her inexperience. At a Q&amp;A at the Academy's theater last Thursday, she fielded a question about the comparisons to Marlowe by saying simply that she had watched <em>Ardent</em> "probably forty times" as a teenager. The room, packed with voters, applauded for a full minute.</p>
<p>Hollow Oak, which has never had a film nominated for best picture, is running a scrappy campaign on a budget that one rival executive described as "roughly what Meridian spends on valet parking." Its founder, Dev Raghunathan, insists the constraint is a feature. "We can't buy this," he says. "We can only screen it. So we're screening it everywhere. Retirement homes, union halls, a bowling alley in Sherman Oaks. If you're a voter and you haven't seen it, that's on you."</p>
<p>Meridian, for its part, is not panicking. A spokesperson noted that <em>The Glass Orchard</em> has yet to open wide and that Marlowe has deliberately limited her campaign appearances. But two people close to the studio said executives had quietly expanded the film's screening schedule and asked Wren, who famously avoids publicity, to sit for a handful of additional interviews in November.</p>
<p>The wider field, meanwhile, is crowded. Rafe Calloway's surprise exit from the <em>Velocity Nine</em> franchise has drawn fresh attention to <em>Harbor Lights</em>, his directorial debut, which premieres at the AFI Fest later this month. And Dame Harriet Quill is said to be considering a return to film after her second season of <em>Sable</em>.</p>
<p>For now, though, the conversation belongs to two women and two very different films: one a European art-house triumph with a fairy-tale subplot, the other an American miniature with nothing but heart. "It's the best kind of race," says the strategist. "Nobody loses. Except, eventually, one of them."</p>`,
