    editorsPick: true,
    body: `<p>About forty minutes into <em>The Glass Orchard</em>, Celeste Marlowe sits at a kitchen table and eats a pear. She eats the whole thing, core and all, in a single unbroken take that lasts almost four minutes. Nothing else happens. A clock ticks. A dog crosses the frame. Somewhere offscreen, a tractor coughs and dies. And by the time she reaches the stem, you understand everything about her character—her grief, her rage, her terrible appetite for a life that has been taken from her—without a single word of dialogue.</p>
<p>That scene will be the one people talk about, and it deserves to be. But it is only one of a dozen moments in Tomasz Wren's film that feel less directed than discovered, as though Wren simply set up his camera in the Loire Valley and waited for something extraordinary to wander past. The result, which won the Palme d'Or in May and opens in New York and Los Angeles this Friday, is the most beautiful film of the year, and the most unsettling.</p>
<p>The plot, such as it is, concerns Hélène, a widow who inherits her husband's failing orchard and refuses, against all financial sense, to sell it to the agribusiness consortium circling the estate. Her stepson wants the money. Her neighbors want the land. The weather, which Wren photographs with the reverence of a medieval painter depicting the wrath of God, seems to want her dead.</p>
<blockquote><p>Wren films decay the way other directors film sex: lingeringly, tenderly, with an almost embarrassing intimacy.</p></blockquote>
<p>Wren films decay the way other directors film sex: lingeringly, tenderly, with an almost embarrassing intimacy. Fruit splits on the branch. Wasps drown in cider. A barn roof slowly gives way over the course of the film, and by the final act, when it finally collapses, the effect is as shattering as any explosion in a summer blockbuster. Cinematographer Anouk Steen shoots almost entirely in natural light, and some interiors are so dark that you lean forward in your seat to make out a face. It is worth the effort.</p>
<p>At the center of it all is Marlowe, who has spent two decades being praised for her restraint and here, finally, lets go. Her Hélène is funny, cruel, sensual and occasionally frightening, a woman who has decided that grief is no excuse for good manners and abandons them with gusto. Watch her face during the scene in which a notary reads the terms of the sale aloud: it travels through contempt, amusement, despair and something close to joy in under a minute, and none of it feels like acting.</p>
<p>The film is not without flaws. At 162 minutes, it tests even the most devoted cinephile's patience, and a subplot involving Hélène's estranged sister, played by the French stage actress Léa Moreau, never quite earns its screen time. Wren's refusal to explain anything—the source of the orchard's mysterious glass ornaments is never revealed—will infuriate as many viewers as it enchants.</p>
<p>But these are quibbles about a work of genuine ambition. In an era of franchise fatigue and algorithmically optimized content, <em>The Glass Orchard</em> is defiantly, gloriously unoptimized. It does not care whether you like it. It only cares whether you are paying attention.</p>
<p>I have seen it three times now. Each time, the pear scene has made me cry, and each time for a different reason. There is no higher praise I can offer a film, and no better reason to see it on the largest screen you can find.</p>`,
