    hoursAgo: 140,
    body: `<p>Ines Okonkwo-Bell has been famous for roughly eleven weeks, and she is still not entirely sure what to do with her hands. She sits cross-legged on a velvet armchair in a borrowed apartment in Brooklyn Heights, turning a mug of chamomile tea in slow circles, and answers every question as if it were the first time anyone had ever asked it. Her performance in Kit Abernathy's <em>Salt Year</em>, as a teenage oyster farmer holding her family together through one brutal Maine winter, has made her the most talked-about newcomer of the year. We spoke about auditions, oysters, and the art of the polite refusal.</p>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> Let's start with the obvious. How did a barista from Providence end up starring in a Kit Abernathy film?</p>
<p><strong>Ines Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> Honestly? My roommate dared me. There was an open call posted on a community board, and she said, "You do impressions of every customer who comes in. You might as well get paid for it." I went in thinking I'd be an extra. I read one scene, and Kit asked me to read it again, and then again, and then she asked if I knew how to shuck an oyster. I said yes. I did not.</p>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> You learned, presumably.</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> I spent six weeks on a farm in Damariscotta before we shot anything. My hands were destroyed. I still have a scar here, see? The farmers were incredible. They didn't care that I was in a movie. They cared whether I could keep up. That was the best acting lesson I've ever had: just keep up with people who actually know what they're doing.</p>
<figure><img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/ines-okonkwo-bell-interview-2/1200/800" alt="Ines Okonkwo-Bell on the Maine coast"><figcaption>Okonkwo-Bell on location in Damariscotta, Maine, during the filming of <em>Salt Year</em>. <span>Photograph by Hana Mori.</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> The film has been compared to <em>The Glass Orchard</em>, and you to Celeste Marlowe, more or less constantly. How does that feel?</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> Surreal. I watched <em>Ardent</em> when I was fourteen, on a laptop, in the back seat of my mom's car, and I remember thinking, I didn't know you were allowed to be that quiet on screen. She sent me flowers after Telluride. The card said, "Welcome. Don't let them rush you." I keep it on my fridge.</p>
<blockquote><p>She sent me flowers after Telluride. The card said, "Welcome. Don't let them rush you." I keep it on my fridge.</p></blockquote>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> And are they rushing you?</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> Everyone wants a meeting. Everyone wants a coffee. I make coffee for a living, I know how much coffee one person can drink. There was a big superhero thing—I'm not allowed to say which one—and the offer was, I mean, it was life-changing money. My family could use it. But I read the script and I didn't recognize anyone in it. I didn't recognize myself. So I said no. My agent cried a little.</p>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> No regrets?</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> Ask me in five years. Right now I want to do a play. Small theater, eight shows a week, nowhere to hide. Nell Harrow has been very generous with her time, and we've talked about something for next fall. That would be a dream.</p>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> What does your family make of all this?</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> My dad is a high school chemistry teacher, so he's mostly concerned with whether I'm eating vegetables. My mom has a Google alert on my name and calls me every morning to read me the comments, which I have begged her not to do. My grandmother in Lagos thinks I'm a newsreader. We've stopped correcting her. It's easier.</p>
<p><strong>Voirtales:</strong> Last question. If you win in March, what's the first thing you'll do?</p>
<p><strong>Okonkwo-Bell:</strong> Go back to Damariscotta. I promised the farmers I'd work a shift. They're holding me to it.</p>`,
