    topStory: true,
    body: `<p>For fifteen years and nine films, Rafe Calloway has jumped out of helicopters, driven sports cars off the tops of parking garages and outrun more fireballs than any actor alive. On Monday afternoon, in a forty-minute meeting at Meridian Pictures' Burbank headquarters, he did something that by all accounts shocked the people in the room even more: he said no.</p>
<p>Calloway, 47, has informed the studio that he will not star in <em>Velocity Ten</em>, the next installment of the franchise that made him one of the most bankable stars in the world, according to three people with direct knowledge of the meeting. The decision forfeits a guaranteed payday of roughly $40 million plus back-end participation, and leaves Meridian scrambling to rethink its most valuable property just as the studio faces a takeover bid from the streaming giant Northlight.</p>
<p>"He was very calm, very gracious," said one person who was present. "He thanked everybody. He said he'd given the series everything he had, and that he didn't have anything left that he could honestly give. Then he shook hands and left. There was about thirty seconds of silence after the door closed."</p>
<blockquote><p>He said he'd given the series everything he had. Then he shook hands and left. There was about thirty seconds of silence after the door closed.</p></blockquote>
<p>A representative for Calloway at Crestline Artists confirmed the decision but declined to elaborate. A Meridian spokesperson said in a statement that the studio was "enormously grateful to Rafe for his partnership" and that the <em>Velocity</em> franchise "will continue to thrill audiences for many years to come."</p>
<p>The timing is not accidental. Later this month, Calloway will premiere <em>Harbor Lights</em>, his debut as a director, at AFI Fest in Los Angeles. A spare, intimate drama about a recovering alcoholic fisherman in Nova Scotia, it was made for less than $9 million—roughly what a single <em>Velocity</em> car chase costs—and features Calloway in a supporting role with barely a dozen lines. Early screenings for industry insiders have been rapturous.</p>
<p>"Rafe has wanted to do this for twenty years," says Kit Abernathy, the <em>Salt Year</em> director, who has become a close friend and informal adviser. "Everyone assumed the action stuff was who he was. It was just what he was good at. Those are different things. He's figured that out later than most people, but he's figured it out."</p>
<p>The decision has set off a frantic round of speculation over who might replace him. Two agents said Meridian had already made informal inquiries about several younger stars, though industry veterans doubt the franchise can survive without its anchor. "You don't recast the Velocity guy," said one producer. "You reboot, or you retire it. And nobody wants to retire a three-billion-dollar asset in the middle of a merger."</p>
<p>Indeed, the timing could hardly be worse for Meridian chair Ruth Adler-Kane, who is fighting to preserve the studio's independence and whose case to shareholders rests in part on the strength of its franchises. A person close to her said she was "disappointed but not surprised," and had suspected for months that Calloway's heart was elsewhere.</p>
<p>As for Calloway, friends say he plans to spend the winter in Nova Scotia, where he bought a house during the <em>Harbor Lights</em> shoot. "He's going to fish, and write, and not be chased by anything," said one. "He seems happier than I've ever seen him."</p>`,
