    hoursAgo: 9,
    topStory: true,
    body: `<p>For seventy years, the Bellandi family has kept its quarrels behind the heavy oak doors of its palazzo on Via della Spiga. On Monday morning, that tradition ended. Chiara Bellandi, the elder daughter of the house's octogenarian matriarch, filed suit in a Milan commercial court seeking to block her younger brother Luca's appointment as chief executive of Casa Bellandi, alleging that he had "manipulated" their mother into signing over voting control of the family holding company.</p>
<p>The filing, which <em>Voirtales</em> has reviewed, runs to 84 pages and reads at times less like a legal document than a family novel. It accuses Luca, 46, the house's creative director since 2015, of isolating their mother, Giovanna Bellandi, from other family members, of restricting access to her medical records and of persuading her to sign a revised shareholder agreement during a hospital stay in August. Chiara, 52, who ran the company's commercial operations until she was removed from the board in September, is asking the court to appoint an independent administrator.</p>
<blockquote><p>This was never about money. It's about whether my mother's house survives my brother's ego.</p></blockquote>
<p>"This was never about money," Chiara said in a brief statement released through her lawyers. "It's about whether my mother's house survives my brother's ego. I have tried for two years to resolve this privately. He has left me no choice."</p>
<p>Luca Bellandi's representatives called the allegations "false, painful and deeply regrettable," and said their client had acted at every stage with his mother's full knowledge and support. "Signora Bellandi is in excellent health and full possession of her faculties," the statement said. "She has made her wishes for the future of her company abundantly clear."</p>
<p>Giovanna Bellandi herself, who founded the house in 1956 as a small knitwear workshop in Como and built it into one of Italy's most recognizable luxury brands, has not commented publicly. People close to the family say she has retreated to her villa on the lake and is refusing to take calls from either child.</p>
<p>The dispute could hardly come at a more delicate moment. Casa Bellandi, which remains entirely family-owned, has been the subject of persistent takeover interest from larger luxury groups, most notably the Ferrante Group, whose chairwoman, Isabella Ferrante, has made no secret of her admiration for the brand. Two people familiar with the matter said Ferrante had approached both siblings separately in recent months, and that Chiara in particular was receptive to a sale.</p>
<p>"Isabella is a patient woman," said one banker who has advised on several luxury acquisitions. "She doesn't need to do anything. She just needs to wait for the Bellandis to exhaust themselves. Family fights are expensive, and they always end the same way: somebody sells."</p>
<p>Inside the company, the mood is described as grim. Several senior employees have reportedly been asked to choose sides, and the house's most recent collection, shown in Paris in October, was produced amid what one staff member called "a war zone." Luca's extravagant, baroque designs were well received by critics—some of whom read the collection's defiance as a direct message to his sister—but sales have slowed, and the company has postponed the opening of a planned flagship in Tokyo.</p>
<p>A first hearing is scheduled for early December. Until then, Milan's fashion establishment is watching with a mixture of sympathy and fascination. "Every family business has this moment," said a longtime industry figure who knows both siblings well. "The Bellandis just happen to be having it in public, in very good clothes."</p>`,
