    type: "story",
    heroCaption: "Prince Leopold and Celeste Marlowe at Skarholm Castle on the day their engagement was announced.",
    heroCredit: "Photograph by Pieter van Lint",
    hoursAgo: 26,
    topStory: true,
    issue: "november-2026",
    body: `<p>Prince Leopold of Esterhaven and the actress Celeste Marlowe will marry on Saturday, May 15, at St. Brigid's Cathedral in Lindholm, the palace announced on Monday, ending months of speculation about the date and setting in motion what is likely to be the most closely watched royal wedding in Europe in a generation.</p>
<p>The ceremony will be followed by a carriage procession through the old city and a reception at Lindholm Palace hosted by Crown Prince Alaric and Crown Princess Margaux, the announcement said. It did not say whether King Frederick III, who has not been seen in public since August, would attend, saying only that "His Majesty is delighted by the news."</p>
<p>The couple, who met at a charity dinner in Lindholm in 2023 and announced their engagement in June, are said to have chosen the date themselves after discarding several earlier options to avoid clashing with Marlowe's awards-season commitments and the filming schedule of her next project. "They wanted spring," said a friend of the couple. "Celeste said she'd spent enough of her life in winter films. She wanted lilacs."</p>
<blockquote><p>Celeste said she'd spent enough of her life in winter films. She wanted lilacs.</p></blockquote>
<p>Planning for the wedding is already well underway, according to several people familiar with the arrangements, and has already produced at least one skirmish with the palace's notoriously traditional protocol office. Marlowe has asked that the cathedral's choir be joined by a small string ensemble performing music from <em>The Glass Orchard</em>, the Tomasz Wren film that won her the Palme d'Or this year. Courtiers initially objected that film scores had never been played at a royal wedding. The bride, sources say, replied that there was a first time for everything.</p>
<p>"She was perfectly charming about it," one person involved said. "But she wasn't going to lose. The protocol people have met their match."</p>
<p>The guest list, expected to number around 1,800, presents its own challenges. The couple are understood to want a mix of European royalty, Esterhavian public figures and friends from Marlowe's world, which raises delicate questions of seating and precedence. One palace official, half-joking, described the task of placing film stars between grand duchesses as "a diplomatic incident waiting to happen." Wren himself, famous for his dislike of public events, has reportedly agreed to attend on the condition that he not be photographed.</p>
<p>The dress, inevitably, is the subject of intense interest. Marlowe has worn Maison Verlaine at nearly every major appearance this year, and its creative director, Odile Marchand, is widely assumed to be the frontrunner. Neither the house nor the palace would comment. A spokesperson for Ama Serwaa, the young London designer whose gown Marlowe wore to last week's Lindholm Opera Gala, also declined to comment, which in the world of royal wedding speculation is practically an admission.</p>
<p>The wedding comes at a delicate moment for the House of Aldermere. Esterhaven goes to the polls in less than two weeks in an election where the future of the monarchy has become a live issue, and the republican Alliance party has criticized the expense of the celebrations. The palace has said that the costs of the ceremony will be met privately by the royal family, with the state covering only security.</p>
<p>Whether a royal wedding will soften attitudes toward the monarchy or harden them is anyone's guess. In Lindholm on Monday afternoon, at least, the mood seemed festive. Outside the cathedral, a small group of tourists had already gathered to take photographs. A vendor nearby was selling postcards of the couple, at a markup that would have made a studio executive proud.</p>`,
