    slug: "juno-vale-halo-tour-final-night",
    title: "Juno Vale's Halo Tour Was the Biggest in History. Its Final Night Was Bigger",
    dek: "Two years, 186 shows, an estimated $2.4 billion in ticket sales—and one last surprise in front of 90,000 people in London. Inside the end of a pop phenomenon.",
    rubric: "Music",
    section: "culture",
    authors: ["priya-raman"],
    tags: ["Music", "Profiles", "Red Carpet"],
    type: "story",
    heroCaption: "Juno Vale on the final night of the Halo Tour, London.",
    heroCredit: "Photograph by Sam Okeke",
    hoursAgo: 14,
    topStory: true,
    body: `<p>At 10:47 p.m. on Sunday, in front of 90,000 people at a stadium in north London, Juno Vale stopped singing. The music cut out. The lights, which for the previous three and a half hours had pulsed and swept and exploded in every color imaginable, went dark except for a single white spotlight. And Vale, 29, the most successful touring artist in the history of recorded music, sat down at the edge of the stage, dangled her feet over the side and began, in a very small voice, to talk.</p>
<p>"I wrote my first song in my bedroom when I was fourteen," she said. "It was terrible. It was about a boy who didn't know I existed. I played it for my mom and she cried, and I didn't know if she was crying because it was good or because it was so bad. I'm going to play it for you now. It's still terrible. But I wanted it to be the last song."</p>
<p>What followed—a two-minute ballad called "Paper Moon," played on an acoustic guitar with a broken string—will almost certainly be the most-watched concert clip of the year. Within an hour of the show's end, versions shot on fans' phones had been viewed more than eighty million times. By Monday morning, the song, which had never been recorded or released, was being streamed from bootleg uploads faster than anything else in the world.</p>
<blockquote><p>It's still terrible. But I wanted it to be the last song.</p></blockquote>
<p>It was a fittingly intimate end to an unfathomably large undertaking. The Halo Tour, which began in Melbourne in October 2024, played 186 shows across six continents and grossed an estimated $2.4 billion, comfortably the highest total ever recorded. It transformed the economies of the cities it visited, filling hotels, restaurants and airline seats; economists coined the term "Halo effect" to describe the measurable bump in local spending. In some cities, the arrival of Vale's fans registered on seismographs.</p>
<p>Behind the statistics was a performer who, by her own account, nearly didn't make it. In an interview with <em>Voirtales</em> last year, Vale described the tour's first months as "the hardest thing I've ever done," and spoke candidly about exhaustion, vocal strain and a period of anxiety so severe she considered canceling the European leg. She credits a small circle of friends, a vocal coach in Nashville and, she says, "a frankly ridiculous amount of face oil."</p>
<p>The final night's guest list was a who's who of the tour's two years. Celeste Marlowe and Prince Leopold watched from a private box, as did Dame Harriet Quill, a friend since Vale guest-starred on the first season of <em>Sable</em>. The supermodel Saskia Brandt, who photographed the tour's official book, was spotted in the pit. Ines Okonkwo-Bell, the <em>Salt Year</em> star, was seen singing every word.</p>
<p>What comes next is unclear. Vale has said she intends to take "at least a year" off and has hinted at a long-gestating project with the director Kit Abernathy, though neither will confirm details. Her label, which has reportedly offered her a new contract worth more than $500 million, is said to be hoping for an album by 2028.</p>
<p>Vale herself, speaking briefly to reporters as she left the stadium in the early hours of Monday, seemed in no hurry. "I'm going to sleep," she said. "For a long time. And then I'm going to write some more terrible songs." She grinned. "Some of them might even be good."</p>`,
