    hoursAgo: 22,
    topStory: true,
    body: `<p>Every Sunday at noon, wherever in the world they happen to be, the three adult children of Augustus Pemberton are expected to join a video call with their father. The calls, which have taken place every week for nearly fifteen years, follow a rigid format. Each child reports on the business they oversee. Their father asks questions, often pointed ones. Then he delivers what one person familiar with the ritual describes as "a short lecture on what everyone is doing wrong." The calls rarely last more than forty minutes. They are, according to the same person, "absolutely terrifying."</p>
<p>For years, the Sunday calls were the main forum in which the Pemberton children competed for their father's approval—and, by extension, for control of Pemberton Media, the $60 billion empire of newspapers, cable channels and digital properties that Gus Pemberton built from a single regional paper in Ohio. But as the patriarch enters his ninth decade, and as the company reels from the fallout of the <em>Capitol Ledger</em> scandal, the competition has moved out of the Sunday calls and into the open.</p>
<p>According to more than fifteen people close to the family and the company, the long-simmering rivalry between the three heirs—Caspian, 54, Lydia, 50, and Archie, 41—has reached a critical point, and Pemberton's refusal to name a successor has left the company in a state of what one senior executive describes as "permanent low-level civil war."</p>
<blockquote><p>Gus thinks competition makes people stronger. Maybe it does. It also makes them hate each other.</p></blockquote>
<p>"Gus thinks competition makes people stronger," says one longtime associate of the family. "He's always pitted the kids against each other. He thinks it's how you find out who's tough enough. Maybe it does make people stronger. It also makes them hate each other."</p>
<p>Caspian, the eldest, runs the company's cable division and has long been seen as the presumptive heir. A conservative in his father's mold, he is close to Governor Roland Pike and represented the family at Pike's recent ranch gathering in Texas. But his standing was badly damaged by the <em>Capitol Ledger</em>'s discredited story about Senator Catherine Whitlow, which several people inside the company believe was encouraged, if not directly ordered, by executives loyal to him. Caspian has denied any involvement.</p>
<p>Lydia, who oversees the company's newspapers, has emerged from the scandal with her reputation enhanced. She commissioned the internal review that found "serious failures" at the paper and pushed for the resignation of its editor. She is regarded within the company as the most capable manager of the three, and has cultivated a reputation as a moderate. People close to her say she believes the company's future lies in rebuilding its journalistic credibility, a view that puts her at odds with her elder brother.</p>
<p>Archie, the youngest, has until recently been the family's wild card: a former DJ and failed restaurateur who now runs the company's small but fast-growing streaming division. He has, according to several people, quietly been building alliances with outside investors, including at least one Gulf sovereign wealth fund, and may be positioning himself as a compromise candidate.</p>
<p>Pemberton himself shows no sign of stepping aside. He still arrives at the company's Manhattan headquarters three days a week, reads every major newspaper cover to cover and remains, by all accounts, fully in command of his faculties and his company. Asked at a recent industry event whether he had chosen a successor, he replied, "Yes. Me."</p>
<p>A spokesperson for Pemberton Media said the company had "a robust succession plan in place" and that the Pemberton family was "united in its commitment to the company's future." Asked to describe the plan, the spokesperson declined.</p>
<p>The Sunday calls, meanwhile, continue. Last weekend, according to one person familiar with the conversation, Pemberton opened the call by asking each of his children to name the biggest mistake they had made that week. Nobody answered for a long time. Then Pemberton laughed, said "That's what I thought," and moved on to the cable ratings.</p>`,
