    hoursAgo: 320,
    editorsPick: true,
    body: `<p>The email arrived in Rowan Achterberg's inbox at 6:12 p.m. on a Thursday. It was three paragraphs long and signed by three of Halcyon's seven directors, among them Dr. Mei-Lin Sorensen, the company's co-founder and chief scientist. It informed him that the board would meet the following morning to vote on a resolution converting his supervoting shares into ordinary stock, a move that would strip him of effective control of the company he had founded. It requested that he not discuss the matter with employees "until the board has concluded its deliberations."</p>
<p>Achterberg read it twice, according to a person who was with him at the time. Then he put down his phone, picked up his jacket and walked out of the building without a word. It would be nearly six hours before anyone at Halcyon heard from him again.</p>
<p>What followed was the most dramatic week in the short, extraordinary history of the artificial-intelligence industry: a boardroom revolt that pitted the company's two most important founders against each other, divided its staff, alarmed its investors and very nearly destroyed a company valued at more than $400 billion. This account, based on interviews with more than two dozen current and former Halcyon employees, investors and directors, many of whom spoke on condition of anonymity, reconstructs that week for the first time.</p>
<blockquote><p>It was never about power. It was about whether anyone was allowed to tell Rowan to slow down.</p></blockquote>
<p>The roots of the crisis went back more than a year. Sorensen, a soft-spoken computational neuroscientist who had co-founded Halcyon with Achterberg and Dr. Ana Kirsch, had grown increasingly concerned that the company was releasing new models faster than its safety teams could evaluate them. She had raised her concerns repeatedly, in memos to the board and in private conversations with Achterberg. "It was never about power," says one person close to her. "It was about whether anyone was allowed to tell Rowan to slow down. And the answer, it turned out, was no."</p>
<p>By Thursday night, word of the board's plan had leaked. By midnight, Halcyon's internal message boards were in uproar. At 3:04 a.m., an engineer on the company's infrastructure team posted a short letter addressed to the board, declaring that the signatories would resign if Achterberg's control of the company was curtailed. By dawn, it had more than four hundred signatures. By Friday afternoon, more than nine hundred.</p>
<p>The board meeting, held by video call on Friday morning, lasted four hours and was, according to two people who were present, "brutal." Sorensen laid out her case in detail. Achterberg, who had returned to the office shortly after midnight, responded with what one director described as "the best speech I have ever heard him give, and he wasn't trying." The vote was postponed.</p>
<p>Over the weekend, the company's major investors weighed in. Several made clear, in private calls with directors, that they would not support any move against Achterberg. One threatened legal action. By Sunday evening, one of the three directors who had signed the original email had withdrawn her support. On Monday morning, the resolution was formally abandoned. On Tuesday, Sorensen resigned.</p>
<p>In the months since, Halcyon has done its best to present the episode as a healthy disagreement among colleagues. Achterberg has praised Sorensen publicly and has announced an expansion of the company's safety research. But the wounds remain raw. Several senior researchers have followed Sorensen to Tessellate, the rival company she founded in the spring. And the question she raised—whether anyone can tell the most powerful man in artificial intelligence to slow down—has not gone away.</p>
<p>"Rowan won," says one former Halcyon executive. "But I'm not sure the rest of us did. If the people who built the thing can't stop it, who can?"</p>`,
