    heroCaption: "A private island in the Caribbean.",
    heroCredit: "Photograph by Rafael Ortiz-Mena",
    hoursAgo: 2100,
    issue: "june-2026",
    body: `<p>For the better part of two decades, the superyacht was the ultimate symbol of extreme wealth. The bigger the boat, the bigger the man, or so the logic went, and a generation of billionaires competed with escalating fervor to commission ever larger, ever more elaborate vessels—some of them longer than a football field, equipped with helipads, submarines, cinemas and, in at least one case, a fully functional snow room.</p>
<p>No longer. According to brokers, architects, lawyers and wealth managers interviewed for this article, the superyacht has fallen sharply out of favor among the world's richest people. Orders for new vessels over 300 feet have declined by nearly half in the past three years. Several of the largest yachts in the world are quietly for sale. And the money that once went into floating palaces is flowing instead into something much more solid: private islands.</p>
<p>"A yacht is a press release," says one London-based broker who has sold several of the world's largest vessels. "Everyone can see it. Everyone can track it. There are websites that tell you where every big boat in the world is at any given moment, and who owns it. If you're a billionaire in 2026, that's the last thing you want. An island is the opposite. Nobody knows it's yours. Nobody can see you. Nobody can come."</p>
<blockquote><p>A yacht is a press release. An island is the opposite. Nobody knows it's yours. Nobody can come.</p></blockquote>
<p>The shift reflects a broader change in the mood of the very rich. After years of public scrutiny—over taxes, over climate emissions, over the political influence of billionaires—many of the world's wealthiest people have grown wary of conspicuous consumption. Superyachts, which are both highly visible and notoriously polluting, have become a particular target. Several were impounded by governments in recent years for reasons ranging from sanctions to unpaid wages, and the experience has left a lasting impression.</p>
<p>Islands, by contrast, offer privacy, security and a degree of permanence. They also, increasingly, offer a kind of status that a yacht cannot. "An island is a legacy," says the broker. "You can leave it to your grandchildren. You can build a house that will last a hundred years. A yacht is out of date the moment it's launched. Someone always builds a bigger one."</p>
<p>The market has responded accordingly. Prices for private islands in the Caribbean, the Pacific and the Mediterranean have risen sharply, and brokers report waiting lists of buyers for the most desirable properties. A small island in the Bahamas with a single house and a private airstrip sold last year for a reported $145 million, a record for the region. In the Baltic, several islets in the archipelago off Esterhaven have changed hands in recent months, prompting a debate in the kingdom's parliament about foreign ownership of coastal land.</p>
<p>Not everyone is convinced the trend will last. "The very rich are like teenagers," says one wealth manager who advises several of the richest families in America. "They all want the same thing at the same time, and then they all get bored of it at the same time. Five years ago it was yachts. Now it's islands. In five years it'll probably be space stations." He pauses. "Actually, some of them are already asking about space stations."</p>
<p>In the meantime, the island boom shows no sign of abating. One prominent technology founder, who asked not to be named, recently completed a vast compound on a Pacific atoll, including a research laboratory, an organic farm and a bunker capable of sustaining forty people for two years. Asked why, he gave the answer that more and more of his peers seem to share. "Because I can," he said. "And because nobody can find me there."</p>`,
